var o=[{title:`Reading the ratio`,text:`A 1:3 interval could be 20 seconds of effort and 60 seconds of recovery, a 1:1 interval could be 30 seconds of each, and 2:1 could be 40 seconds of work and 20 seconds easy. The ratio alone does not describe the workout until you know the duration and intensity.`},{title:`Long recovery for quality`,text:`Fairly long rests help you keep your speed or power across short hard efforts. A 1:3 setup can suit someone practicing faster running or a demanding movement who wants to avoid tiring out right away. Very intense sprinting can need far more recovery than 1:3 to keep output up.`},{title:`Equal or short recovery`,text:`At 1:1, work and recovery take the same time. At 2:1, recovery is shorter, so fatigue can build quickly. These formats can work for moderate circuits, but they are not automatically better for advanced endurance or strength goals.`},{title:`Adjust for ability and exercise`,text:`A beginner might walk briskly for 30 seconds and easily for 90 seconds, then slowly lengthen the work. A trained cyclist may use longer hard efforts with generous recovery. Jumping movements often need more technical control, so choose rest that keeps your landings good. Judge progress by whether you finish with good technique, lengthen the rest when you need it, and do not make every interval harder just to shorten the workout.`}];var n=[{label:`ACE: designing high-intensity intervals`,url:`https://www.acefitness.org/continuing-education/certified/may-2021/7844/how-to-use-the-ace-ift-model-to-design-effective-hiit-workouts/`},{label:`CDC measuring activity intensity and the talk test`,url:`https://www.cdc.gov/physicalactivity/basics/measuring/index.html`},{label:`CDC adult physical activity recommendations`,url:`https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/guidelines/adults.html`}];var r={sections:o,sources:n};export{r as default,o as sections,n as sources};